---
import Container from '@/components/Container.astro'
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro'
import { representingTasteProject } from '@/research/representing-taste'
import { structProject } from '@/research/struct-project'
---

<Layout
  title={structProject.title}
  description={structProject.summary}
  canonicalPath={structProject.path}
>
  <Container class="flex flex-col gap-y-10 pb-24">
    <header class="flex flex-col gap-y-4 border-b pb-8">
      <a href="/research/" class="text-sm text-muted-foreground hover:underline"
        >← Research</a
      >
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">{structProject.status}</p>
      <h1 class="max-w-3xl text-3xl font-bold">{structProject.title}</h1>
      <p class="max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">{structProject.summary}</p>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-3 text-sm">
        <a
          class="font-semibold underline underline-offset-4"
          href={structProject.demoPath}>Try the demo →</a
        >
        <a
          class="underline underline-offset-4"
          href={representingTasteProject.path}>REPResenting Taste</a
        >
        <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href={structProject.repository}
          >Source code</a
        >
        <a
          class="underline underline-offset-4"
          href="/research/semantic-responsive-typesetting/"
          >Early SRT working note</a
        >
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="prose max-w-[70ch] dark:prose-invert">
      <h2>Role in REPResenting Taste</h2>
      <p>
        Struct is a supporting component of <a
          href={representingTasteProject.path}>REPResenting Taste</a
        >. It supplies portable document semantics and rendering capabilities
        within its existing ownership boundary; it does not supply a claim that
        the programme’s proposed decision representation is novel or effective.
      </p>
      <p>
        Its existing PDF-import and responsive-reading demo is the programme’s
        first milestone and remains available at <a
          href={structProject.demoPath}>the live Struct demo</a
        >. The demo is an experimental import path for review, not a result of
        the planned composition-and-revision study.
      </p>

      <h2>Struct research question</h2>
      <p>{structProject.question}</p>
      <p>
        The proposition is that the document remains a semantic graph. Pages,
        columns, and line breaks are renditions computed for a reading surface.
        Stable object identities connect headings, prose, figures, captions,
        references, and annotations across those renditions.
      </p>

      <h2>Current research direction</h2>
      <p>
        The October 2026 proposal is a reproducible fidelity evaluation loop:
        compare version-matched PDFs and source HTML with generated Struct,
        XHTML, and EPUB; keep text flow, figures, tables, equations, references,
        and navigation separately accountable; and record localized regressions
        across reading profiles.
      </p>
      <p>
        The adapter is available as an experimental converter. The unified
        comparison harness, independently adjudicated challenge corpus, and
        visual-fidelity work are proposals in progress. A successful conversion,
        a valid EPUB, or a model score alone does not establish faithful
        reconstruction.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://github.com/erniesg/struct/issues/25"
            >Reproducible evaluation and regression harness</a
          >: start with an independently reviewed pilot, freeze the evidence,
          and compare baseline and candidate outputs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://github.com/erniesg/struct/issues/26"
            >Calibrated text-only evaluation</a
          >: advisory model judgments that require calibration and cannot
          measure visual or table fidelity.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://github.com/erniesg/struct/issues/27"
            >Feedback-derived challenge corpus</a
          >: separate reported hypotheses, adjudicated cases, and held-out
          evaluation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://github.com/erniesg/struct/issues/28"
            >Contents hierarchy and navigation</a
          >, and <a href="https://github.com/erniesg/struct/issues/29"
            >panels and table styling</a
          >: preserve source-supported structure and appearance, with
          reproducible fixtures.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Implementation</h2>
      <p>
        Struct supplies a source-neutral document model, validation, and
        deterministic XHTML and reflowable EPUB rendering. Its reference PDF
        adapter uses Docling and PDF text, glyph, and link evidence to
        reconstruct a document before rendering. PDF reconstruction and semantic
        rendering are separate steps: a valid output file can still contain
        extraction errors.
      </p>
      <p>
        The public demo exposes the reference PDF-to-EPUB path. The broader
        research also considers finite-height composition, source provenance,
        reader position, and annotation preservation. Trying the converter does
        not establish that all of those hypotheses have been evaluated.
      </p>

      <h2>Evidence so far</h2>
      <p>
        The September adapter work reported 155 of 156 papers passing its
        applicable source-derived reader checks across four local corpora. That
        is a historical implementation report, not independent validation of
        visual fidelity; the original reports were local artifacts and the
        proposed unified harness remains separate. <a
          href="https://github.com/erniesg/struct/pull/13"
          >Adapter report and qualifications</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p>
        The original engineering benchmark used <strong
          >one trusted structured fixture with 11 nodes, one relationship, and
          two annotations</strong
        >, across mobile, Paper Pro Move, Paper Pro, and print profiles. It
        recorded preservation of the supplied structure, relationship,
        annotations, and anchors, with no detected clipping or overlap in that
        fixture.
      </p>
      <p>
        These are fixture-level engineering checks, not a corpus result or
        reader study. The recorded timings measure local composition after
        module initialization, not PDF extraction or end-to-end conversion
        latency.
      </p>
      <p>
        A separate documented frozen-ten PDF run covered 495 pages. All ten
        documents remained <strong>review-required</strong>; producing
        deterministic, valid EPUBs did not make them publication-ready.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="/research/struct/resources/reader-criteria.md"
            >Reader-facing success criteria and corpus evaluation proposal</a
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/research/struct/resources/benchmark-report.md"
            >Engineering benchmark report</a
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/research/struct/resources/pdf-ingestion-benchmark.md"
            >PDF ingestion benchmark and qualifications</a
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/research/struct/resources/limitations.md"
            >Recorded evaluation limitations</a
          >
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Earlier proposal and prior work</h2>
      <p>
        The proposal investigates an integrated system for faithful composition
        across heterogeneous reading surfaces. Adaptive layout, PDF reflow, and
        annotation anchoring each have substantial prior art. The literature
        review is a targeted scoping review, prepared in July 2026, rather than
        a systematic review or proof of novelty.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="/research/struct/resources/proposal.md"
            >Proposal and implementation scope</a
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/research/struct/resources/literature-review.md"
            >Literature review</a
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/research/struct/resources/references.bib" download
            >References (BibTeX)</a
          >
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Limitations and next questions</h2>
      <p>
        Complex tables, equations, reading order, scans, and figure-caption
        associations can require correction or fall back to image regions.
        Automated checks do not replace comparison with the original source.
        Outputs from the demo are for review, rather than automatically approved
        publications.
      </p>
      <p>
        Further work needs independent source truth, comparisons with relevant
        baselines, varied document and device evidence, and reader evaluation.
        The package and public publishing lifecycle remain development work;
        this page does not claim a registry release or a peer-reviewed result.
      </p>

      <h2>Try it</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={structProject.demoPath}
          >Explore existing samples or convert your own PDF.</a
        > Supplied samples are freely readable. Server conversions use a daily allowance
        shared by visitors on the same network.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Container>
</Layout>
